.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt EPOLL 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm epoll_create ,
.Nm epoll_create1 ,
.Nm epoll_ctl ,
.Nm epoll_wait ,
.Nm epoll_pwait ,
.Nm epoll_pwait2
.Nd watch a set of descriptors
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/epoll.h
.Ft int
.Fn epoll_create1 "int flags"
.Ft int
.Fn epoll_ctl "int epfd" "int op" "int fd" "struct epoll_event *event"
.Ft int
.Fn epoll_wait "int epfd" "struct epoll_event *events" "int maxevents" "int timeout"
.Ft int
.Fn epoll_pwait "int epfd" "struct epoll_event *events" "int maxevents" "int timeout" "const sigset_t *sigmask"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
An epoll set is a descriptor that keeps the descriptors a process
watches, so that a wait names them once rather than in every call, as
.Xr poll 2
does.
.Fn epoll_create1 ,
number 329, makes one;
.Fa flags
is 0 or
.Dv EPOLL_CLOEXEC .
.Fn epoll_create ,
number 254, is the older form, whose argument must be positive and is
otherwise ignored.
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /fd
names the set
.Dq anon_inode:[eventpoll] .
.Pp
.Fn epoll_ctl ,
number 255, changes the set:
.Dv EPOLL_CTL_ADD
adds
.Fa fd
with the events in
.Fa event
and its word of data,
.Dv EPOLL_CTL_MOD
changes them, and
.Dv EPOLL_CTL_DEL
removes
.Fa fd .
The events are those of
.Xr poll 2 ,
.Dv EPOLLIN ,
.Dv EPOLLOUT ,
.Dv EPOLLRDHUP
and the rest;
.Dv EPOLLERR
and
.Dv EPOLLHUP
are always reported, for a registration of no events too.
A registration is level-triggered: a wait reports it while its
descriptor is ready.
With
.Dv EPOLLET
it is edge-triggered: a wait reports it when an event appears, or when
the descriptor has changed since its last report, as a pipe drained and
filled again between two waits has, and not again until then.
A change is a wake of the descriptor's wait queue, of whatever kind, so
an edge may be reported where Linux, which looks at the events of the
change, reports none.
With
.Dv EPOLLONESHOT
the first report disables it, errors and hangups included, until an
.Dv EPOLL_CTL_MOD .
.Dv EPOLLEXCLUSIVE
is accepted at
.Dv EPOLL_CTL_ADD
and changes nothing, since each wait here wakes its own process.
.Pp
.Fn epoll_wait ,
number 256, waits until a descriptor of the set is ready, a signal
arrives, or
.Fa timeout
milliseconds pass, a negative
.Fa timeout
waiting without a limit, and stores at most
.Fa maxevents
events and their data in
.Fa events .
.Fn epoll_pwait ,
number 319, waits with the signal mask
.Fa sigmask
in place, as
.Xr ppoll 2
does.
.Fn epoll_pwait2 ,
number 441, takes its timeout as a
.Vt struct timespec
of 64-bit seconds and nanoseconds; negative seconds are
.Er EINVAL ,
and a timeout longer than 2147483647 milliseconds, about 24.8 days, is
shortened to that.
A timeout is rounded up to whole 10 ms ticks.
.Pp
A registration belongs to the descriptor number and the open file it
named when it was added.
When the number is closed, or names another open file, the next wait
drops the registration; Linux keeps it while the open file stays open
through another descriptor.
A set inside another set is reported ready by what it holds, but a
change in it does not wake a wait on the outer set.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn epoll_create1
returns the new descriptor,
.Fn epoll_ctl
0, and the waits the number of events stored, 0 when the time ran out;
each returns -1 with
.Va errno
set on failure.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width EEXIST
.It Er EBADF
.Fa epfd
or
.Fa fd
is not an open descriptor.
.It Er EEXIST
.Dv EPOLL_CTL_ADD
of a descriptor already in the set.
.It Er EFAULT
.Fa event
or
.Fa events
is not in the caller's memory.
A wait that cannot store an event returns the events it stored before
it, or this error when there are none; the events it could not store
are reported again by a later wait, one-shot and edge-triggered ones
among them, as in Linux.
.It Er EINTR
A signal arrived before a descriptor was ready.
.It Er EINVAL
.Fa epfd
is not an epoll set, or is
.Fa fd ;
.Fa op
or
.Fa flags
is not one of those above;
.Fa maxevents
is not positive;
.Dv EPOLL_CTL_MOD
touched
.Dv EPOLLEXCLUSIVE ;
or the timeout of
.Fn epoll_pwait2
has negative seconds, or nanoseconds outside 0 to 999999999.
.It Er ENOENT
.Dv EPOLL_CTL_MOD
or
.Dv EPOLL_CTL_DEL
of a descriptor not in the set.
.It Er EPERM
.Fa fd
is a regular file or a directory, which is always ready.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr eventfd 2 ,
.Xr poll 2 ,
.Xr select 2
